WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Film Courage: Shaun I’m hoping we can go to page&nbsp;
one of your script for A CALIFORNIA CHRISTMAS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.040
I’m curious what you like about the opening&nbsp;
scene of the movie which is page one of course.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.520
Shaun Paul Piccinino, Filmmaker: The opening is&nbsp;
obviously meant to establish Joseph’s character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.880
(played by Josh Swickard) and he is kind&nbsp;
of living this playboy lifestyle, big city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.560
wealthy. Obviously he’s got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:40.240
someone in bed. He’s young and he’s kind of using&nbsp;
obviously his status to kind of live carefree in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:48.160
that way. So basically the point of it is to set&nbsp;
up his character for his arc throughout the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.880
because obviously he needs to grow. He’s not&nbsp;
the best of guys at this point. So it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.280
helps illustrate that point. It sets up who he&nbsp;
is and the journey he is going to have to go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.200
whether he likes it or not is basically the idea.&nbsp;
Obviously you won’t know all of that while seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.360
this first scene but it sets it up of who he is&nbsp;
and where he is at at this point in his life.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.960
Film Courage: And he’s not a total&nbsp;
jerk. He offers to buy the woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.280
room service. So you’ve&nbsp;
got to give him some props.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.680
Shaun: Yeah…yeah..again I think with those&nbsp;
little details is that we don’t want people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.400
to just completely hate him right off the&nbsp;
bat. And we did have some other things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.640
in early versions and stuff that were like&nbsp;
yeah that might make him too much of a jerk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.840
Certainly there is an argument there that he&nbsp;
is still a jerk but you know what I am saying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:48.160
He has to have some underlying qualities and&nbsp;
redeemable pieces that you want to see him go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.400
on a journey of growth and self-growth&nbsp;
and becoming hopefully a better person.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.920
Film Courage: So before we meet&nbsp;
Joseph the main character we have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.480
aerial shot. Can we talk&nbsp;
about the establishing image?

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.960
Shaun: Yes the very first shot is kind of&nbsp;
coming up off of the water and revealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:13.200
San Francisco which that is where he is and&nbsp;
where his family business is. So kind of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:20.320
establishing that whole Bay Area big city and&nbsp;
the hustle and bustle of city life because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:27.280
is obviously a stark contrast to the ranch that he&nbsp;
later goes to and ends up spending a lot of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.760
We also did some interesting things in color&nbsp;
there. So the city life has a little bit cooler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.720
of tones to them and when we go to the ranch&nbsp;
everything becomes a little bit warmer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:48.960
earthier in the tones so that’s like a little&nbsp;
details that we pay attention to when we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.760
making it. Probably most people will never notice&nbsp;
it but even with that opening shot we wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:00.480
the color palette of it to feel cooler in&nbsp;
tone versus the later part of the movie.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.640
Film Courage: And also too he&nbsp;
leaves one woman in the hotel room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:12.000
and then as he leaves he kind of flirts&nbsp;
with a desk clerk and you see her sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.360
of…not unprofessionally but it already sets&nbsp;
him up to be sort of this Casanova type.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:24.880
Shaun: Correct, yes. He’s definitely got that air&nbsp;
about him and you get the sense that you know…

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:25.507
Film Courage: He doesn’t hear “no” a lot.

00:03:25.507 --> 00:03:29.920
Shaun: He doesn’t hear “no” a lot.&nbsp;
It’s that hashtag “best life,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.680
right? He’s living his best&nbsp;
life. But you know anyways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.480
obviously there is more to it than that&nbsp;
and at his core he’s searching for more.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.520
Film Courage: Sure. What do you think&nbsp;
the hook is of the first page that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.640
people want to keep reading?

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.920
Shaun: It goes just beyond&nbsp;
the first page I guess but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:57.280
the hook of the first page is really the kind of&nbsp;
setting and the character. For me when I read it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:03.600
I wanted to know more about him so it’s&nbsp;
really like why is he this way? What makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:12.320
him…especially because he’s outright just turns&nbsp;
the girl down for a second date right there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.520
makes you go well that’s audacious. What is&nbsp;
his story? So hopefully that’s the hook. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.760
was for me when I read it because I didn’t&nbsp;
write it Lauren Swickard did [she also stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.840
as Callie in A CALIFORNIA CHRISTMAS] and when I&nbsp;
was sent it that’s what grabbed me right away.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.560
Film Courage: And I think he&nbsp;
says “I’ll call you” or whatever.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.120
Shaun: And she says “No you won’t.” Exactly.&nbsp;
That little piece right there I’m like okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.680
and the fact that he can get away&nbsp;
with that I think there’s probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.160
plenty of people who are like Well I&nbsp;
wouldn’t be able to get away with that or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:51.200
those kind of things. It makes it intriguing and&nbsp;
of course you see the relationship walking through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:58.240
the hotel lobby, the people like him. He’s got&nbsp;
that thing about him, his charm, his charisma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.320
It always makes it more interesting and&nbsp;
I think even in the back of my head I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.200
was even initially right away thinking oh&nbsp;
he is going to have some lessons to learn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:13.520
You know what I mean? He’s probably&nbsp;
going to have to go on a journey.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.920
Film Courage: And I’m sorry he is brought&nbsp;
in to be (and I hope this isn’t a spoiler)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.240
like a “honey pot,” to kind of like infiltrate&nbsp;
and seduce someone he needs a signature from?

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.080
Shaun: So basically his company and his mother&nbsp;
who runs the company they haven’t been able to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:38.560
the owners of this ranch to sell which they need&nbsp;
to build a shipping facility for their corporation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:46.800
(their business) and so his mom decides that&nbsp;
her son basically doesn’t do anything…you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.520
know…he’s got a title and works at the company but&nbsp;
what does he actually do? She recognized that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:06:00.000
has a certain skill and it’s a younger woman who&nbsp;
owns the ranch and so she says let’s put your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.320
actual skills to use. Go out there and get this&nbsp;
girl to sell. Get this young woman to sell. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.120
I think he thinks he’s going to be&nbsp;
able to waltz in there and get his way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:21.440
and get the yes that he always hears and&nbsp;
I can say it doesn’t go as he has planned.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.560
Film Courage: Sure and his&nbsp;
[character] arc evolves from there.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.440
Shaun: There is some case of missing identity&nbsp;
as well or mis-identity (not missing)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.040
that happens for him as well in&nbsp;
there that kind of starts him down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.280
a totally different journey than he&nbsp;
thought he was going to be going on.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.320
Film Courage: Looking back on it&nbsp;
now what mistakes do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.840
were in the first page or was this the actual&nbsp;
shooting script and there were no mistakes?

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:56.720
Shaun: Oh well this is definitely not the&nbsp;
first version of the script for sure so this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.720
closer to the shooting [version]. I can’t remember&nbsp;
if what I sent you is the actual shooting one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:10.400
and gosh it’s hard to remember if there&nbsp;
was. I think there was more time taken in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.480
earlier drafts which I think took&nbsp;
too long to get to those pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.880
and it is not even just the first page but that&nbsp;
was part of it. I think it got more refined and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.960
kind of picked the moments that actually mattered&nbsp;
to convey the message that we’re trying to convey.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.800
Film Courage: Is this still&nbsp;
the opening of the movie?

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.960
Shaun: Yes, this is still&nbsp;
the opening of the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.760
So yes it stayed. Obviously I think it’s&nbsp;
important again because we have to establish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:50.320
our character’s journey. So Joseph and also Callie&nbsp;
they both have their journeys and their journeys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:08:01.440
intersect and they help each other on their&nbsp;
journey so that’s the age-old kind of love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:11.840
twist. Their journeys intersect and of course they&nbsp;
need each other to kind of complete their journey.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.437
Question for the Viewers: How is this helpful&nbsp;
to you? What else should we have covered?

